PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A}

The trp attenuator has four regions capable of alternative RNA hairpins. In high tryptophan, the ribosome translates trpL without stalling, covering segments 1 and 2; this leaves 3 free to pair with 4 and form the functional terminator hairpin. A mutation that blocks 1–2 pairing frees segment 2 even after ribosome release, allowing the competing 2–3 antiterminator to form instead. Consequently the 3–4 terminator cannot assemble and transcription continues.  

B blocks the antiterminator, so 3–4 forms constitutively.  
C alters the poly-U tract after the hairpin, not the stem-loop itself.  
D and E affect expression level but do not change hairpin choice.  

Thus only A directly prevents terminator formation under repressing conditions.}

